<chapter
 id="lecture12-10">
#title("Example: A Bit More of Animation")

#para("\

With continuation, we can readily insert break points into the
execution of a program so as to support some interactive form of
program execution (e.g., animation).

")

#para("\

Hanoi Towers is a puzzle that is often chosen to illustrate recursion
and exponentiation.

Given three poles: Pole 1, Pole 2 and Pole 3, there are 64 disks of
distinct sizes stacked on Pole 1 such that no disk is stacked on one
of a lesser size, and both Pole 2 and Pole 3 are empty. The player is
asked to finish the task of moving all the disks from Pole 1 to Pole 2
(while using Pole 3 as a spare): Only one disk can be moved from one
pole to another one at any time and no disk is ever allowed to be
stacked on another one of a lesser size during the entire process of
disk-moving.\

")

#para("\

With recursion, it is quite straightforward for one to infer that
2#sup('64')-1 moves are needed in order to accomplish the aforementioned task.
For instance, the following code shows how this task can be done. The function
#dyncode("move") is the one that makes a move (in the code of this chapter, it counts the
moves and prints a line), and the number of the disks is a parameter of
the other function:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
typedef pole = int // 1, 2 or 3
//
var theMoves_var: int = 0 // the number of the moves
val theMoves =
ref_make_viewptr{int}(view@ theMoves_var | addr@ theMoves_var)
//
fun
move(src: pole, dst: pole): void =
(
!theMoves := !theMoves + 1;
println! ("move ", !theMoves, ": a disk from pole ", src, " to pole ", dst)
)
//
')

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
nmove(n: int, src: pole, dst: pole, tmp: pole): void
//

//
implement
nmove
( n
, src, dst, tmp) =
if
(n > 0)
then
(
nmove(n-1, src, tmp, dst); move(src, dst); nmove(n-1, tmp, dst, src);
)
// end of [if] // end of [nmove]
//
')

Given two poles #dyncode("src") and #dyncode("dst"), the function
#dyncode("move") moves the top disk at
pole #dyncode("src") and stack it on the top of the disks at pole
#dyncode("dst").  Given a natural number #dyncode("n") and three poles
#dyncode("src"), #dyncode("dst"), and #dyncode("tmp"), the function
#dyncode("nmove") moves the top #dyncode("n") disks at pole
#dyncode("src") and stack them on the top of the disks at pole
#dyncode("dst") while using pole #dyncode("tmp") as the spare.  Assume
that #dyncode("n") is greater than 0.  In order to move the top
#dyncode("n") disks from pole #dyncode("src") to pole #dyncode("dst"),
#dyncode("nmove") first moves the top #dyncode("n-1") disks from pole
#dyncode("src") to pole #dyncode("tmp") and then calls
#dyncode("move") to move 1 disk from pole #dyncode("src") to pole
#dyncode("dist") and then move the top #dyncode("n-1") disks from pole
#dyncode("tmp") to pole #dyncode("dst"). Note that #dyncode("nmove")
eventually makes #dyncode('2#sup("n")-1') calls to #dyncode("move")
for each given natural number #dyncode("n").\

")

#para("\

Suppose that we want to animate the process of disk-moving incurred
by calling #dyncode("nmove"). A very natural approach that we can take
is to first translate #dyncode("nmove") (implemented in the direct-style)
into the following #dyncode("k_nmove") implemented in the CPS-style. The
continuations are the linear closures of the previous chapter (of the type
#stacode("cont0"), which is the type of the thunks that are called once and
then freed by #dyncode("cont0_call")):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
k_move(src: pole, dst: pole, k: cont0): void
//
extern
fun
k_nmove(n: int, src: pole, dst: pole, tmp: pole, k: cont0): void
//

//
implement
k_nmove
( n
, src, dst, tmp
, k0 ) =
if
(n > 0)
then
(
k_nmove
( n-1, src, tmp, dst
, llam() => k_move(src, dst, llam() => k_nmove(n-1, tmp, dst, src, k0)))
)
else cont0_call(k0)
// end of [if] // end of [k_nmove]
//
')

")

#para("\

Usually, the function #dyncode("k_move") is expected to be implemented
in the follow way:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// the usual implementation of [k_move]
//
implement
k_move
(src, dst, k0) = let
  val () = move(src, dst) in cont0_call(k0)
end // end of [k_move]
//
val () = println! ("-- direct style --")
val () = nmove(3, 1, 2, 3)
//
val () = !theMoves := 0
val () = println! ("-- CPS-style --")
val () = k_nmove(3, 1, 2, 3, llam() => println! ("done"))
//
')

After calling #dyncode("move") to create some effects,
#dyncode("k_move") calls the passed continuation immediately to
initiate the work to be continued. For the sake of animation, the
function #dyncode("k_move") can be implemented as follows (it is given the name
#dyncode("k_move2"), and the function #dyncode("k_nmove2") is
#dyncode("k_nmove") calling it):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The version of [k_move] for the animation
//
extern
fun
k_move2(src: pole, dst: pole, k: cont0): void
//
implement
k_move2
(src, dst, k0) = let
  val () = move(src, dst) in save_cont(k0)
end // end of [k_move2]
//

//
// [k_nmove2] is [k_nmove] in which [k_move2] is called
//
extern
fun
k_nmove2(n: int, src: pole, dst: pole, tmp: pole, k: cont0): void
//
implement
k_nmove2
( n
, src, dst, tmp
, k0 ) =
if
(n > 0)
then
(
k_nmove2
( n-1, src, tmp, dst
, llam() => k_move2(src, dst, llam() => k_nmove2(n-1, tmp, dst, src, k0)))
)
else cont0_call(k0)
// end of [if] // end of [k_nmove2]
//
')

where the function #dyncode("save_cont") stores a given continuation
somewhere so that it can be fetched and then called at a chosen point
in future. The continuation is linear and it is stored (as a pointer) in a toplevel
variable, from which the function #dyncode("step") fetches it and calls it (the function
returns false if there is no continuation to continue with, and the function
#dyncode("steps") calls it until there is none):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The continuation is saved, instead of being called: the next
// step of the animation is taken when [step] is called.
//
var theSaved_var: ptr = the_null_ptr
val theSaved =
ref_make_viewptr{ptr}(view@ theSaved_var | addr@ theSaved_var)
//
fun
save_cont(k0: cont0): void =
!theSaved := $UNSAFE.castvwtp0{ptr}(k0)
//
// [step] returns false if there is no continuation to continue with
//
fun
step(): bool = let
  val p = !theSaved
in
  if iseqz(p)
    then false
    else let
      val () = !theSaved := the_null_ptr
      val () = cont0_call($UNSAFE.castvwtp0{cont0}(p))
    in
      true
    end // end of [else]
end // end of [step]
//
fun
steps(): void = if step() then steps() else ()
//
')

The following code shows the animation: after the first call to
#dyncode("k_nmove2") returns, only the first move has been made, and
the rest of the work is the continuation that was saved by
#dyncode("k_move2"); each call to #dyncode("step") makes the next move:

#dats2xhtml('\
implement
main0() = {
//
val () = !theMoves := 0
val () = println! ("-- animation: one step at a time --")
//
// the first call returns after the first move
//
val () = k_nmove2(3, 1, 2, 3, llam() => println! ("done"))
val () = println! ("(the first move is made, the rest is saved)")
//
val-true = step()
val-true = step()
//
val () = println! ("(two more moves are made, the rest is saved)")
//
val () = steps()
//
val () = println! ("the number of the moves: ", !theMoves)
//
} (* end of [main0] *)
')

")

#para("\

In a browser, the saved continuation would be called by a handler of a click or
by a timer (see #dyncode("execute_after") in the chapter on animation), and the
GC-era demo that animates the process of disk-moving involved in solving
the puzzle of Hanoi Towers is <ulink
url=\"https://ats-lang.github.io/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/12-10/CODE/HanoiTowers.html\">on-line</ulink>.

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/12-10/CODE", "on-line") the entirety
of the code used in this chapter. The file #filename("HanoiTowers.dats") was compiled with
#command("patscc") (no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer,
which reports no leak. The three versions print the same 7 moves for 3 disks.

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture12-10"-->

